From 777c7846bcb4cebcd8869a9d1914e9aedaa5dfea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 19:19:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Stop excusing genuine low pronunciation scores as measurement
 noise
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The "accuracy sits far below fluency/prosody" guard in PronunciationService
was built for one specific failure: scripted assessment scored against our
own (sometimes wrong) transcript, where a bad transcript drags accuracy down
alone. That guard stayed active after the service switched to unscripted
assessment by default (the actual fix for that failure) and kept firing
there for a different reason entirely — Azure's own fluency score runs
lenient and routinely sits well above a mediocre accuracy score, so the gap
tripped on ordinary "fluent but mispronouncing" speakers, not just broken
measurements. Every time it fired, the marker was told to disregard a real
low accuracy number. That is the generous-pronunciation bug.

- isAccuracySuspect() (now a pure, exported, unit-tested function) only
  fires in scripted mode, where the transcript-dependency it was built for
  can actually occur. In unscripted mode a gap between accuracy and
  fluency/prosody is real evidence about the speaker, not an artifact, and
  must reach the marker.
- AIEvaluationService's marking prompt gets explicit accuracy-to-descriptor
  guidance (80+/60-79/below 60, mirroring the fluency guidance's concrete
  thresholds) and an explicit instruction that a confident, fluent delivery
  does not excuse a low accuracy score — they measure different things.
- test/pronunciation.test.js covers the scoping: unscripted never suspects
  regardless of gap size, scripted still catches the original calibration
  case, a small ordinary gap is never flagged, and a missing prosody score
  is handled.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LgKXYQSxMY9AyY5uyuhSbh
